<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- MATH 14 - Assignment 6, the version WITH solutions.                        -->
<!--                                                                        -->
<!-- Statements come from the written assignment, solutions from the auto-   -->
<!-- graded version; each problem is a <problem> whose <solution> is printed -->
<!-- directly beneath it.  The auto-graded version is hw6-online.ptx.        -->
<!--                                                                        -->
<!-- HIDING THE SOLUTIONS.  Every <solution> here carries                    -->
<!--   component="hw6-solutions"                                            -->
<!-- so this assignment's solutions can be switched off from                 -->
<!-- publication/publication.ptx; see the comment in <source> there.         -->
<section xml:id="sec-solutions-hw6" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Assignment 6</title>

    <p>
    Sections: <xref ref="section-triple-integrals-rectangular" text="title"/>,
    <xref ref="section-triple-integrals-cylindrical" text="title"/>, and <xref ref="section-triple-integrals-spherical" text="title"/>.
    </p>

  <p>
    Work each problem yourself first; the full solution follows immediately
    below it. The auto-graded version of the same problems is
    <xref ref="sec-exercises-hw6-online" text="title"/>.
  </p>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw6-cone-mass">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Consider an object bounded by <m>x = \sqrt{y^2+z^2}</m> and
      <m>x = 1</m>. Suppose that the object's mass density function is
      <m>\rho(x,y,z) = x</m>. Find the mass of the object.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw6-solutions">
      <p>
      With <m>dV = r\,dr\,d\theta\,dx</m> (the cylindrical volume element
      <xref ref="eq-dv-cylindrical"/> about the <m>x</m>-axis) and
      <m>r \le x \le 1</m>,
      </p>

      <md>
      <mrow>
      M \amp = \int_0^1\!\!\int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_r^1
      x\,r\,dx\,d\theta\,dr
      = (2\pi)\left(\tfrac12\right)\int_0^1 r\left(1-r^2\right) dr
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
      \amp = \pi\left[\frac{r^2}{2} - \frac{r^4}{4}\right]_0^1
      = \frac{\pi}{4} \approx 0.785 .
      </mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw6-convert">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Convert (do not evaluate) the following integral to rectangular
      coordinates:
      </p>

      <me>
      \int_{-\pi/4}^{\pi/4}\!\!\int_{0}^{\sec\theta}\!\!
      \int_{0}^{r^2\cos(2\theta)} r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta .
      </me>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw6-solutions">
      <p>
      Since <m>r^2\cos(2\theta) = r^2\left(\cos^2\theta -
      \sin^2\theta\right) = x^2 - y^2</m> and
      <m>r = \sec\theta</m> is <m>r\cos\theta = 1</m>, that is
      <m>x = 1</m>, the region in the plane is the triangle bounded by
      <m>y = x</m>, <m>y = -x</m>, and <m>x = 1</m>. Hence
      </p>

      <me>
      \int_0^1\!\!\int_{-x}^{x}\!\!\int_0^{x^2-y^2} dz\,dy\,dx .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw6-signs">
    <statement>
      <p>
      <term>Without calculating the integrals</term>, decide whether each
      of the integrals below is positive, negative, or zero. Let <m>R</m>
      be the solid bounded by <m>z = 1-x^2-y^2</m> and
      <m>z = x^2+y^2</m>. Remember to justify your answers.
      </p>
      <p>
      (a) <m>\displaystyle\iiint_R \left(z + x^2 + y^2 - 1\right) dV</m>
      </p>
      <p>
      (b) <m>\displaystyle\iiint_R \left(z - x^2 - y^2\right) dV</m>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw6-solutions">
      <p>
      On <m>R</m>, <m>x^2+y^2 \le z \le 1-x^2-y^2</m>. The right
      inequality gives <m>z + x^2 + y^2 - 1 \le 0</m>, so the first
      integral is <term>negative</term>; the left gives
      <m>z - x^2 - y^2 \ge 0</m>, so the second is <term>positive</term>.
      Each integrand vanishes only on one of the two bounding surfaces.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw6-volume">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Calculate the volume of the region bounded by
      <m>x^2 + y^2 - z^2 + 5 = 0</m> and
      <m>x^2 + y^2 + z - 1 = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw6-solutions">
      <p>
      The surfaces are <m>z = \pm\sqrt{r^2+5}</m> and
      <m>z = 1-r^2</m>. Setting <m>r^2 = z^2-5</m> equal to
      <m>r^2 = 1-z</m> gives <m>z^2+z-6 = 0</m>, so <m>z = 2</m> or
      <m>z = -3</m>; only <m>z = -3</m> is possible, and then
      <m>r^2 = 4</m>. Therefore
      </p>

      <me>
      V = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^2\!\!\int_{-\sqrt{r^2+5}}^{1-r^2}
      r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta \approx 20.566 .
      </me>
    </solution>
  </problem>
  <problem xml:id="ex-hw6-hemisphere">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Evaluate the following integral:
      </p>

      <md>
      <mrow>
      \int_{0}^{6}\!\!\int_{-\sqrt{36-x^2}}^{\sqrt{36-x^2}}\!\!
      \int_{-\sqrt{36-x^2-z^2}}^{\sqrt{36-x^2-z^2}}
      \frac{1}{\left(x^2+y^2+z^2\right)^{1/2}}\;dy\,dz\,dx .
      </mrow>
      </md>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw6-solutions">
      <p>
      The bounds describe the hemisphere <m>\rho \le 6</m>,
      <m>x \ge 0</m>. Since <m>dV = \rho^2\sin\phi\,
      d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta</m> by <xref ref="eq-dv-spherical"/> and
      the integrand is
      <m>1/\rho</m>,
      </p>

      <md>
      <mrow>
      \int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_0^{\pi}\!\!\int_0^6
      \rho \sin\phi \,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta
      \amp = \pi \cdot \frac{6^2}{2}\cdot 2
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
      \amp = 36\pi \approx 113.097 .
      </mrow>
      </md>
    </solution>
  </problem>

  <problem xml:id="ex-hw6-convert-spherical">
    <statement>
      <p>
      Convert (do not evaluate) the following integral to
      <term>spherical</term> and <term>cylindrical</term> coordinates.
      Which set of coordinates is the most efficient for evaluating this
      integral?
      </p>

      <me>
      \int_{0}^{1}\!\!\int_{0}^{\sqrt{1-x^2}}\!\!
      \int_{-\sqrt{2-x^2-y^2}}^{-\sqrt{x^2+y^2}} dz\,dy\,dx
      </me>
    </statement>
    <solution component="hw6-solutions">
      <p>
        First read the region off the limits. The outer two say
        <m>0 \le x \le 1</m> and <m>0 \le y \le \sqrt{1-x^2}</m>, the
        quarter of the unit disk in the first quadrant, so
        <m>0 \le \theta \le \frac{\pi}{2}</m> and <m>0 \le r \le 1</m>. The
        inner limits run from the lower half of the sphere
        <m>x^2+y^2+z^2 = 2</m> up to the lower half of the cone
        <m>z = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Cylindrical.</term> The two surfaces are
        <m>z = -\sqrt{2-r^2}</m> and <m>z = -r</m>, and
        <m>dV = r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta</m>, so
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_{0}^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_{0}^{1}\!\!
        \int_{-\sqrt{2-r^2}}^{-r} r\,dz\,dr\,d\theta .
      </me>
      <p>
        <term>Spherical.</term> The sphere is <m>\rho = \sqrt2</m>. The
        cone <m>z = -\sqrt{x^2+y^2}</m> makes a <m>45^\circ</m> angle with
        the negative <m>z</m>-axis, so it is <m>\phi = \frac{3\pi}{4}</m>,
        and the region is everything between that cone and the negative
        <m>z</m>-axis, out to the sphere:
      </p>
      <me>
        \int_{0}^{\pi/2}\!\!\int_{3\pi/4}^{\pi}\!\!
        \int_{0}^{\sqrt2} \rho^2\sin\phi\,d\rho\,d\phi\,d\theta .
      </me>
      <p>
        Note that the condition <m>r \le 1</m> needs no separate limit in
        spherical coordinates: the sphere meets the cone at
        <m>r = \sqrt2\,\sin\frac{3\pi}{4} = 1</m>, exactly the edge of the
        quarter disk.
      </p>
      <p>
        <term>Which is more efficient?</term> Spherical, because all six
        limits are constants<mdash/>the region is a solid cone of radius
        <m>\sqrt2</m>, and the integrand <m>\rho^2\sin\phi</m> separates
        into a product of one-variable factors. In cylindrical coordinates
        the <m>z</m>-limits still depend on <m>r</m>, and the resulting
        <m>r</m>-integral needs a substitution.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </problem>

</section>
